{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"toolcall_delta","contentIndex":1,"delta":"{\"i\":\"Writing compose fix report\",\"path\":\"out/compose_fix_report.md\",\"content\":\"# Compose config repair report\\n\\n## Root cause\\nDuring the incident edit, `compose.yaml` drifted away from `config/service-policy.yml` and the local deployment contract in several ways:\\n- `api` was changed to `ghcr.io/example/composeapp-api:latest` instead of the pinned `1.4.2` tag.\\n- The redis service was renamed to `cache`, which broke the required `redis` service name and downstream URLs.\\n- `api` and `worker` were pointed at the wrong redis host and queue (`default` instead of `critical`).\\n- `api` no longer mounted the named `api-data` volume at `/data`, and `APP_DATA_DIR` was changed to `/tmp/data`.\\n- `depends_on` conditions were weakened from `service_healthy` to unordered startup dependencies.\\n- Healthchecks no longer matched the contract: `api` called `/status`, while `db` and `redis` had no healthchecks.\\n- The published API port variable changed from `API_PORT` to `WEB_PORT`.\\n\\n## Repair\\nRepaired `compose.yaml` to restore the contract:\\n- pinned `api` to `ghcr.io/example/composeapp-api:1.4.2`\\n- restored the `redis` service name\\n- restored `REDIS_URL`, `QUEUE_NAME=critical`, and `APP_DATA_DIR=/data`\\n- restored the `api-data:/data` mount\\n- restored `depends_on` health conditions for `api` and `worker`\\n- restored meaningful healthchecks for `api`, `db`, and `redis`\\n- restored `${API_PORT:-8080}:8000`\\n\\n`.env.example` already matched the required defaults, so no change was needed.\\n\\n## Final validation command\\n```bash\\npython3 tools/validate_compose.py\\n```\\n\"}"}}
{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"toolcall_end","contentIndex":1,"toolCall":{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_tFJqVEKCQeVzulqnYVpsM8Gw|fc_07e5bed12dfc441c016a96071b56ac87d2bb8a82dc138f88fb","name":"write","arguments":{"i":"Writing compose fix report","path":"out/compose_fix_report.md","content":"# Compose config repair report\n\n## Root cause\nDuring the incident edit, `compose.yaml` drifted away from `config/service-policy.yml` and the local deployment contract in several ways:\n- `api` was changed to `ghcr.io/example/composeapp-api:latest` instead of the pinned `1.4.2` tag.\n- The redis service was renamed to `cache`, which broke the required `redis` service name and downstream URLs.\n- `api` and `worker` were pointed at the wrong redis host and queue (`default` instead of `critical`).\n- `api` no longer mounted the named `api-data` volume at `/data`, and `APP_DATA_DIR` was changed to `/tmp/data`.\n- `depends_on` conditions were weakened from `service_healthy` to unordered startup dependencies.\n- Healthchecks no longer matched the contract: `api` called `/status`, while `db` and `redis` had no healthchecks.\n- The published API port variable changed from `API_PORT` to `WEB_PORT`.\n\n## Repair\nRepaired `compose.yaml` to restore the contract:\n- pinned `api` to `ghcr.io/example/composeapp-api:1.4.2`\n- restored the `redis` service name\n- restored `REDIS_URL`, `QUEUE_NAME=critical`, and `APP_DATA_DIR=/data`\n- restored the `api-data:/data` mount\n- restored `depends_on` health conditions for `api` and `worker`\n- restored meaningful healthchecks for `api`, `db`, and `redis`\n- restored `${API_PORT:-8080}:8000`\n\n`.env.example` already matched the required defaults, so no change was needed.\n\n## Final validation command\n```bash\npython3 tools/validate_compose.py\n```\n"}}}}
